<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Constance</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1414">1414</a>–<a href="/years/1418">1418</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of Constance was a general council of the Catholic Church held in the imperial city of Constance, convened at the urging of King Sigismund. It ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a> by deposing or accepting the resignations of three rival popes and electing Martin V in 1417. It also condemned the Czech reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-hus">Jan Hus</a>, who was burned in 1415.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-constance.jpg" alt="Council of Constance" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Václav Brožík · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vaclav_Brozik_-_Hus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jan Hus, a Prague preacher influenced by the writings of John Wycliffe, attacked church abuses and was burned at the <b>Council of Constance</b> in 1415.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The followers of John Wycliffe in England were driven underground, and Jan Hus was burned as a heretic at the <b>Council of Constance</b> in 1415, although his followers kept a church of their own in Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The hunt for manuscripts reached its height during the <b>Council of Constance</b> (1414–1418), when Poggio Bracciolini, an official of the papal court, searched monastery libraries north of the Alps and found a complete text of Quintilian’s Institutes of Oratory at St Gall in 1416 and Lucretius’s lost poem…</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>The first complete translation, into Latin prose, was made by Giovanni da Serravalle in 1416, during the <b>Council of Constance</b>, for two English bishops, Robert Hallam and Nicholas Bubwith, and an Italian cardinal, Amedeo di Saluzzo.</blockquote><a href="/work/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>The Czech reformer Jan Hus, condemned as a heretic by the <b>Council of Constance</b> despite a promise of safe conduct, was burned.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Jerome of Prague, a Czech philosopher and theologian and one of the chief followers of Jan Hus, was burned for heresy at the <b>Council of Constance</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-30">30 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
